hting fixed while changing material, then hold material fixed while changing lighting; inspect shadows and lit surfaces separately. Check object transforms, UV orientation and seams; an unwrapped UV workflow may be needed for different faces. Compare at final image resolution. These visual checks have not been executed on the development images.\n\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Local coordinates and nonuniform object scale change apparent world frequency; different objects may not align. Noise is not a physically accurate grain model, and texture complexity is not proof of better resemblance. A single image cannot uniquely separate albedo from lighting; exposure and color management matter. Material creation modifies Blender data and the caller must manage assignment and cleanup.\n\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General shader and coordinate knowledge, prompted by the RGB appearance goals. No GT-derived shader claims or transfer results.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n**Applicable Conditions:** Candidate mesh scenes with hypothesized horizontal supports and semantic assemblies.\n\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Loose props, disconnected components and penetrations can undermine an otherwise convincing view. A support relation is directional and differs from parenting or constraints; neither AABB overlap nor parenting proves contact. Axis-aligned bounds are a coarse screen, not surface collision geometry.\n\n**Executable Method:** In Blender call `support_diagnostic(child,support,tolerance)` on evaluated mesh objects, with nonnegative tolerance in scene world units. The returned dict contains vertical AABB gap and XY overlap when available. Positive gap means a gap between bounding extremes; near-zero means these extremes nearly coincide; possible penetration means vertical bounding intervals overlap beyond tolerance; unknown support covers absent XY overlap or unavailable mesh geometry. Sloped surfaces, overhangs and 